The '''History of Sarta (1197–1313)''' describes the formation of the Sartan kingdom from a set of Sartan-speaking polities in Illypnia, its expansion under the House of Fuertelon, and the consolidation of the northern highlands, the southern marches, and the mountain frontier. The period began with the unification of the Sartan people in 1197, when [[Ramiro I of Fuertelon|Ramiro I]] established the [[House of Fuertelon]] as the first royal house of Sarta, and ended in 1313, when the Great Catholic Schism produced conflict between Petran and Zamarran ecclesiastical alignments. Church authorities generally disapproved of the warfare, although some supported consolidation under the Fuertelon crown as a means of reducing fragmentation among Sartan-speaking polities.

== Unification of Sarta ==

Before unification, Sartan-speaking communities were divided among highland polities, coastal towns, and tributary kingdoms in the south. These communities shared a language and related elite traditions, but they were politically fragmented and often competed over trade routes, mountain passes, and access to the coast. The [[Kingdom of Fuertelon]], based in the northern highlands, emerged as the strongest of the inland polities and initiated the unification process in 1197.

[[Ramiro I of Fuertelon|Ramiro I]], the first king of Sarta, ruled through the House of Fuertelon and established hereditary primogeniture as the basis of succession. Fuertelon’s rulers built their authority around warfare, mountain defense, and control of the routes linking the highlands to the coast. The early unification was not a single event but a sequence of campaigns and oaths that brought neighboring Sartan-speaking territories under a common crown. By the end of the first phase of expansion, Sarta had become a recognizable kingdom rather than a loose regional bloc.

== Kings of the House of Fuertelon ==

The early kingdom is usually divided into four broad reigns. The exact dates are approximate, but the sequence captures the main political phases of the period.

