== The Sartan Wars ==

The Sartan Wars were the sequence of campaigns that expanded Fuertelon’s core into a wider Sartan kingdom. They secured the northern highlands and extended Sartan authority into the lowland and foothill regions. The wars were shaped by terrain: mountain forts, narrow passes, and river corridors mattered more than large set-piece battles, and control of roads often proved more important than control of individual towns.

The kingdom’s earliest expansion focused on bringing the southern kingdoms, including the [[Kingdom of Hirona]], into the Sartan sphere. The conquest of Hirona marked the end of the first Sartan Wars and fixed the Sierra Madre as the main dividing line of the kingdom. Beyond the range lay the [[Kingdom of Kastruneto]] and the [[Kingdom of Tamarin]]. Tamarin was also Sartan-speaking and remained a major rival in the south.

By the mid-13th century, the southern frontier had settled on the Sierra Madre line, roughly the same divide that still cuts modern Sarta in half. The kingdom’s southern advance reached as far as [[Kingdom of Hirona|Hirona]], which became a long-term marker for the boundary between the northern crown lands and the more contested southern marches.

== Conquests in the south ==

The southern campaigns were the most important part of Sarta’s early state formation. The lowland kingdoms south of Fuertelon controlled fertile valleys, market towns, and the routes that linked the interior to the coast. Their rulers were generally weaker than Fuertelon’s highland kings, but they were often protected by local alliances and by their relationship to Jacan.

Sartan conquest in the south followed a pattern of pressure, submission, and selective incorporation. Some kingdoms were absorbed directly after defeat, while others retained local rulers in exchange for tribute and military service. This created a layered political order in which the crown in Fuertelon sat above a patchwork of marcher lords, tributary kings, and coastal administrators.

The result was a kingdom that was no longer purely highland. By the mid-13th century, Sarta had become a north-south state with a highland core, a southern chain of dependent polities, and a growing interest in the ports that linked the kingdom to broader Illypnian trade.

== The Sartan-Tamarin War ==

From 1247 to 1273, Sarta fought a war with the [[Kingdom of Tamarin]], a Sartan-speaking polity south of the Sierra Madre and north of the territory that Jacan sought to influence along the [[Rio Grande]]. The conflict concerned territorial control and influence over the river region.

Tamarin was gradually annexed into Sarta over the course of the war. The campaign brought the Sartan frontier farther south and increased the kingdom’s control over the routes that connected the highlands to the river corridor. [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]] had backed Tamarin early in the conflict, seeking a division of the territory along the Rio Grande, with Jacan to the south and Sarta to the north.

In 1273, after Tamarin's annexation was effectively complete, Sarta signed a treaty with Jacan. The agreement recognised the settlement and established the frontier along the river corridor: Jacan to the south, Sarta to the north.

== Wars with the Sabean Confederacy ==

To the northwest, Sarta also faced repeated conflict with the [[Sabean Confederacy]], a confederacy of city-states in the area of modern [[Sabea]]. These wars were less about outright annexation than about control of passes, herding land, and the margins of the mountain frontier. The Sabean city-states remained difficult to defeat decisively, and campaigns there tended to be seasonal, punitive, and uneven in result.

The northwest frontier mattered because it connected Sarta to the wider politics of western Illypnia. In that direction lay [[Volisania]], which was already an important regional power and commercial horizon. Even when Volisania was not directly at war with Sarta, its influence shaped trade routes, diplomatic expectations, and the balance of power in the northwest.

The Sabean wars also helped define Sartan rulership. Kings who could hold the high passes and keep the frontier stable were regarded as strong rulers, even when they did not win much territory. In practice, this made the northern state more durable than expansive: Sarta’s early rulers learned to value control and tribute as much as conquest.

== Jacan and the coastal kingdoms ==

Sarta’s relationship with [[Jacan]] remained important throughout this period. Jacan’s influence over the coast shaped Sarta’s southern politics, especially where coastal kingdoms had already aligned themselves with Jacan as tributaries or clients. The southern wars therefore also concerned access to the sea and the loyalty of intermediate rulers.

Rather than displacing Jacan’s influence entirely, the Sartan crown often operated alongside it. Some coastal polities changed allegiance as Sarta grew stronger; others remained formally tied to Jacan while recognising Sartan authority on the ground. The south remained a politically mixed zone.

By the end of the 13th century, Sarta was a stable kingdom of moderate size in Illypnia. It was large enough to matter in regional politics, strong enough to dominate its immediate neighbors, and exposed enough to remain dependent on alliances, marriages, and church politics beyond its borders.

== The Great Catholic Schism ==

The [[Great Catholic Schism]] of 1313 grew out of a wider dispute over church authority, taxation, and jurisdiction in Illypnia. By the early 14th century, ecclesiastical power was concentrated in the papal states around [[Volisania]], while several kingdoms to the south and east regarded papal demands as increasingly burdensome. In [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]], a landlocked kingdom with weaker access to the main church revenues and trade routes, criticism of indulgences, purgatory, and the Curia had already gained support among the crown and its allies.

After the death of the sitting pope, the conclave elected the Kastrunetean [[Augustin V]] and he refused to relocate the papal court to the papal states. Instead, he established his court at [[Zamarra]] on [[Lake Zamarra]]. The decision created a permanent rival centre of ecclesiastical authority and set the stage for the [[Two Centuries War]], which began in 1337. [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]], [[Kingdom of Breisland|Breisland]], and [[Kingdom of Nabia|Nabia]] supported the Zamarran side, while [[Volisania]], [[Sarta]], and [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]] supported the papal states.

In Sarta, the schism affected politics because the kingdom had existing ties to both papal and Zamarran-aligned networks. The north remained more closely linked to Petran Catholic authority, while the south had stronger ecclesiastical and trade connections to Jacan and the Zamarran sphere. The conflict did not create Sarta’s divisions, but it reinforced them and placed the kingdom within a broader regional struggle that continued into the Two Centuries War.

